3:   "proof": "Fix p, K, \u03bb and an admissible lattice M as in the target, and put R=Z_(p). Use the complex basis e_i=y^i v/i! (0\u2264i\u2264\u03bb). The divided-power formulas are X_r e_i=binom(\u03bb-i+r,r)e_(i-r) and Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r), with out-of-range terms zero, where X_r=x^r/r! and Y_r=y^r/r!. In particular these operators have integral matrices in this basis. Regard M_R=R\u2297_Z M as a full R-lattice in V(\u03bb), and let A_R be the image of U_Z\u2297_Z R acting on V(\u03bb). Admissibility implies A_R M_R\u2286M_R.\n\nThe formulas give Y_\u03bbX_\u03bb=E_\u03bb\u03bb, and X_(\u03bb-i)E_\u03bb\u03bb=E_i\u03bb. Also E_\u03bb\u03bbY_(\u03bb-j)=binom(\u03bb,j)E_\u03bbj. Since \u03bb<p, binom(\u03bb,j) is a unit in R for every j; hence all E_\u03bbj and E_ij=E_i\u03bbE_\u03bbj belong to A_R. These are identities of complex-linear operators on V(\u03bb), and thus preserve M_R. (For \u03bb=0 the identity operator is the sole matrix unit and the same conclusions below apply.)\n\nFor each i define the R-submodule J_i={c\u2208C : c e_i\u2208M_R}. It is finitely generated: M_R is finitely generated over R, and E_ii M_R=J_i e_i, so J_i e_i is the image of a finitely generated module under E_ii. It is nonzero: since M_R spans V(\u03bb) over C, E_ii M_R spans the nonzero line C e_i over C. Applying E_ij to c e_j shows J_j\u2286J_i, and applying E_ji gives the reverse inclusion. Thus all J_i equal one nonzero finitely generated R-submodule J of C. For each m\u2208M_R, the equality m=\u03a3_i E_ii m shows that each of its coordinate components lies in J e_i. Conversely, each J e_i is contained in M_R by definition. Therefore M_R=\u2295_(i=0)^\u03bb J e_i. As M_R is free of rank \u03bb+1 over the discrete valuation ring R, this decomposition shows that J is a torsion-free R-module of rank one; equivalently it is a free rank-one R-module. In particular dim_(F_p)(J/pJ)=1. The decomposition identifies M_R/pM_R, with its operators x,y,h, with the standard reduced module having basis the images of e_0,\u2026,e_\u03bb: choose any R-module isomorphism R\u2245J and use it in each summand. Localization and reduction give M_R/pM_R\u2245M\u2297_Z F_p, and extension of scalars gives the claimed identification after tensoring with K.\n\nFinally, on the standard reduced module, for every r\u2264\u03bb<p, X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of compositions of x and y, respectively, because r! is invertible modulo p. The matrix-unit identities above use only these divided powers and coefficients binom(\u03bb,j), all nonzero in F_p. Thus the operators generated by x and y include every E_ij, also in characteristic 2. Any L(K)-submodule is stable under x,y and their compositions, hence under every E_ij. If it contains a nonzero vector, some coordinate c_i is nonzero; applying E_ji gives c_i e_j for each j, and scalar closure then gives every e_j. The submodule is the whole space. The module has dimension \u03bb+1>0. This proves irreducibility for every stated M and K, including \u03bb=0 (where it is one-dimensional).",
